WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='1704174c-c925-5996-879c-67382bd32ba2' AND gt.achievement_id='739bdba4-0fac-4455-9ee2-1a56096cab92');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '2dd2eaeb-4401-5197-8be0-824f1b85bce4','Boxiness carve','A corrective EQ cut in the low-midrange, typically around 400-800 Hz, that reduces the hollow, ''cardboard-box'' coloration heard in dialogue or vocals. Carving this region opens up the sound and removes small-room or resonance buildup without thinning the voice''s fundamental body.','Boxy sound is a hollow, cardboard-box tone in a voice. It sits in the low mids, around 400 to 800 Hz. A boxiness carve is an EQ cut in that range. Removing it makes dialogue sound more open and clear.','It removes low-mid resonance that makes a voice sound closed-in or ''boxy,'' improving openness and helping dialogue blend across scenes recorded in different spaces.','A dialogue editor sweeps a narrow EQ boost to find the boxy resonance around 500 Hz, then cuts a few dB there to clean up a room-heavy line.','Dialogue EQ',ARRAY['Articulation lift','Chest reduction','Brittle consonants','Resonance','Dialogue stem','EQ']::text[],ARRAY['Cutting too wide or too deep in the low mids, which drains warmth and leaves the voice thin.','Confusing boxiness (low mids) with muddiness lower down or harshness higher up, and cutting the wrong band.','UNSAFE: Monitoring the dubbing stage at high SPL for long stretches while fine-tuning the mix risks permanent noise-induced hearing loss; keep exposure within OSHA limits (90 dBA time-weighted over 8 hours, 85 dBA action level) and take listening breaks.']::text[],ARRAY['Cleaning up dialogue recorded in a small, reflective room.','Matching the tone of ADR to production sound with less room coloration.','Reducing resonance on a lav mic placed under clothing.']::text[],'739bdba4-0fac-4455-9ee2-1a56096cab92'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='2dd2eaeb-4401-5197-8be0-824f1b85bce4' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Boxiness carve'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '2dd2eaeb-4401-5197-8be0-824f1b85bce4','739bdba4-0fac-4455-9ee2-1a56096cab92',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='2dd2eaeb-4401-5197-8be0-824f1b85bce4' AND gt.achievement_id='739bdba4-0fac-4455-9ee2-1a56096cab92');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'f19ce093-1aea-5583-82c0-e4d9038f5f59','Breath riding','The manual or automated attenuation of audible breaths between phrases in a dialogue or vocal track, done by riding level automation (or gain edits) rather than deleting the breaths outright. It controls distracting breath noise while leaving enough breath to keep the delivery natural.','Between words, people take breaths that the mic picks up. Breath riding means lowering the volume of those breaths. The mixer turns them down so they do not distract. Some breath is left in so speech still sounds natural.','It tames distracting or exaggerated breaths, especially after compression, while preserving the natural rhythm of speech that fully removing breaths would destroy.','A dialogue mixer draws small level-automation dips on loud in-breaths after heavy compression so they sit back without sounding gated.','Dialogue Automation',ARRAY['Chest reduction','Articulation lift','Compression','Level automation','De-essing','Dialogue stem']::text[],ARRAY['Deleting breaths entirely, which makes speech sound robotic and unnatural.','Riding breaths so hard they cut off abruptly, creating an audible gating pump.','UNSAFE: Monitoring the dubbing stage at high SPL for long stretches while fine-tuning the mix risks permanent noise-induced hearing loss; keep exposure within OSHA limits (90 dBA time-weighted over 8 hours, 85 dBA action level) and take listening breaks.']::text[],ARRAY['Controlling loud breaths on a close-miced narration after compression.','Smoothing an emotional performance where gasps are distracting.','Preparing an audiobook or voiceover track for a clean, even delivery.']::text[],'739bdba4-0fac-4455-9ee2-1a56096cab92'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='f19ce093-1aea-5583-82c0-e4d9038f5f59' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Breath riding'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'f19ce093-1aea-5583-82c0-e4d9038f5f59','739bdba4-0fac-4455-9ee2-1a56096cab92',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='f19ce093-1aea-5583-82c0-e4d9038f5f59' AND gt.achievement_id='739bdba4-0fac-4455-9ee2-1a56096cab92');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '2c2af443-31c2-5d9b-a32d-ea6c1609f84a','Brittle consonants','A descriptor for dialogue in which consonant sounds, especially sibilants and plosives, come across as thin, harsh, and glassy due to excessive or peaky high-frequency energy in roughly the 5-8 kHz-and-up range. It signals an unbalanced upper spectrum that fatigues the listener and can be worsened by over-boosting presence.','Brittle consonants sound thin, harsh, and glassy. It happens when high frequencies around 5 to 8 kHz are too strong. The S and T sounds then feel sharp and tiring to hear. Mixers ease these back so speech sounds smooth.','Naming this defect lets a mixer diagnose harsh, fatiguing consonants and correct them with de-essing or gentle high-frequency shaping instead of more boosting.','A mixer hears brittle S and T sounds on a bright lav mic and applies de-essing plus a small high-shelf cut to smooth them.','Dialogue EQ',ARRAY['Articulation lift','De-essing','Sibilance','Boxiness carve','Presence','Dialogue stem']::text[],ARRAY['Fixing brittle consonants by cutting broadly and dulling the whole voice instead of targeting the harsh band.','Adding more presence boost to ''help clarity,'' which makes the brittleness worse.','UNSAFE: Monitoring the dubbing stage at high SPL for long stretches while fine-tuning the mix risks permanent noise-induced hearing loss; keep exposure within OSHA limits (90 dBA time-weighted over 8 hours, 85 dBA action level) and take listening breaks.']::text[],ARRAY['Taming a bright, close lavalier that exaggerates S sounds.','Correcting over-EQ''d dialogue that was pushed too hard in the presence range.','Smoothing sibilant narration before a fold-down to small speakers.']::text[],'739bdba4-0fac-4455-9ee2-1a56096cab92'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='2c2af443-31c2-5d9b-a32d-ea6c1609f84a' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Brittle consonants'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '2c2af443-31c2-5d9b-a32d-ea6c1609f84a','739bdba4-0fac-4455-9ee2-1a56096cab92',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='2c2af443-31c2-5d9b-a32d-ea6c1609f84a' AND gt.achievement_id='739bdba4-0fac-4455-9ee2-1a56096cab92');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '3d587aab-bb4a-5d50-a7d8-52e6d1bee992','Cascaded dubbing','An automatic dubbing approach built from separate, chained models, typically automatic speech recognition, then machine translation, then text-to-speech, as opposed to a direct end-to-end system. The modular design is simpler to build and reuse, but errors from each stage can propagate and accumulate down the chain.','Cascaded means done in separate steps, one after another. In cascaded dubbing, one tool writes down the speech. A second translates it, and a third speaks it. Each step can add its own errors, so mistakes can build up.','It lets dubbing reuse mature, independently trained components for recognition, translation, and synthesis, at the cost of error propagation that a direct model tries to avoid.','An engineer assembles a dubbing prototype from existing ASR, translation, and TTS services, accepting that a recognition error early on will carry into the spoken output.','Automatic Dubbing',ARRAY['Automatic dubbing','Automated dubbing pipeline','Audio-visual speech-to-speech translation','Text-to-speech','Prosodic alignment']::text[],ARRAY['Assuming cascaded and direct systems perform identically; the cascade is prone to compounding errors.','Blaming the text-to-speech stage for mistakes actually introduced by earlier recognition or translation.','Neglecting timing and prosody handoff between stages, so the final dub drifts from the picture.']::text[],ARRAY['Standing up a quick dubbing prototype from off-the-shelf models.','Comparing a cascaded pipeline against a direct speech-to-speech model.','Diagnosing where an error entered a multi-stage dubbing chain.']::text[],'739bdba4-0fac-4455-9ee2-1a56096cab92'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='3d587aab-bb4a-5d50-a7d8-52e6d1bee992' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Cascaded dubbing'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '3d587aab-bb4a-5d50-a7d8-52e6d1bee992','739bdba4-0fac-4455-9ee2-1a56096cab92',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='3d587aab-bb4a-5d50-a7d8-52e6d1bee992' AND gt.achievement_id='739bdba4-0fac-4455-9ee2-1a56096cab92');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '42d8e3aa-4b5a-5d54-b2c8-15595370fc67','Center-channel anchoring','The practice of routing principal dialogue to the dedicated center loudspeaker in a surround mix so speech stays locked to the screen. Anchoring dialogue in the discrete center channel keeps it stable and intelligible for all seats, independent of the phantom-center imaging that stereo playback relies on.','Surround setups have a speaker in the middle called the center. Center-channel anchoring puts the main dialogue there. This keeps voices locked to the screen no matter where you sit. It stops speech from drifting to the side speakers.','It ties dialogue to the picture and gives every seat a stable, intelligible voice image, which a phantom center from left/right speakers cannot guarantee off-axis.','A re-recording mixer buses the dialogue premix to the center channel in a 5.1 or Atmos mix so voices stay pinned to the screen while effects and music spread around them.','Surround Mixing',ARRAY['ADR seating','Dialogue stem','Fold-down','Print master','Phantom center','5.1 surround']::text[],ARRAY['Spreading dialogue into left and right for ''width,'' which weakens the screen lock and hurts off-center seats.','Forgetting to check the fold-down, where a center-anchored mix collapses differently to stereo or mono.','UNSAFE: Monitoring the dubbing stage at high SPL for long stretches while fine-tuning the mix risks permanent noise-induced hearing loss; keep exposure within OSHA limits (90 dBA time-weighted over 8 hours, 85 dBA action level) and take listening breaks.']::text[],ARRAY['Placing lead dialogue in the center channel of a 5.1 theatrical mix.','Keeping a narrator anchored on screen while effects move around the room.','Checking that center-anchored dialogue survives a stereo fold-down for broadcast.']::text[],'739bdba4-0fac-4455-9ee2-1a56096cab92'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='42d8e3aa-4b5a-5d54-b2c8-15595370fc67' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Center-channel anchoring'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '42d8e3aa-4b5a-5d54-b2c8-15595370fc67','739bdba4-0fac-4455-9ee2-1a56096cab92',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='42d8e3aa-4b5a-5d54-b2c8-15595370fc67' AND gt.achievement_id='739bdba4-0fac-4455-9ee2-1a56096cab92');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'b1b7e2db-a949-574b-b941-676921bf11fc','Chest and head registration','In vocal production, the two principal registers of the human voice: chest register (the lower, fuller sound with strong lower-harmonic resonance felt in the chest) and head register (the lighter, higher sound with resonance sensed in the head), each produced by different vocal-fold behavior. Registration refers to how a voice manages and blends these registers, including the transition (passaggio) between them.','The voice has different gears called registers. Chest voice is the low, full sound you feel in your chest. Head voice is the lighter, higher sound felt up high. Singers blend the two so their range sounds even.','Understanding the two registers lets a singer or vocal producer manage tone and evenness across the range and smooth the break where the registers change.','A vocal producer coaches or comps takes so a singer''s chest and head registers match in tone through the passaggio, avoiding an obvious break on a sustained line.','Vocal Register',ARRAY['Chest reduction','Passaggio','Falsetto','Vocal fold','Resonance','Belting']::text[],ARRAY['Treating chest and head voice as entirely separate voices rather than registers to be blended.','Pushing chest register too high, which strains the voice at the transition instead of shifting registers.','Confusing head register with breathy falsetto, which is a related but distinct production.']::text[],ARRAY['Coaching a singer to smooth the break between chest and head voice.','Comping vocal takes so register tone stays consistent across a chorus.','Diagnosing why a sustained note sounds uneven as the singer shifts register.']::text[],'739bdba4-0fac-4455-9ee2-1a56096cab92'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='b1b7e2db-a949-574b-b941-676921bf11fc' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Chest and head registration'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'b1b7e2db-a949-574b-b941-676921bf11fc','739bdba4-0fac-4455-9ee2-1a56096cab92',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='b1b7e2db-a949-574b-b941-676921bf11fc' AND gt.achievement_id='739bdba4-0fac-4455-9ee2-1a56096cab92');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'c30f307a-ced9-5a67-a203-482f9f18956a','Chest reduction','A corrective EQ move that reduces the low-frequency ''chest'' resonance and body of a voice, roughly in the 100-400 Hz range, to thin a boomy or overly warm tone. It trims proximity-effect buildup and heavy chest resonance so dialogue or vocals sit clearly in a mix without dominating the low end.','The low, warm body of a voice comes from chest frequencies. These sit roughly between 100 and 400 Hz. Chest reduction is an EQ cut in that low range. It thins a boomy voice so it sounds clearer in the mix.','It controls excess low-frequency body so a voice does not sound boomy or muddy and does not crowd out other low-end elements in the mix.','A mixer cuts a few dB around 150-250 Hz on a close-miced voice to remove proximity-effect boom before setting it into the dialogue premix.','Dialogue EQ',ARRAY['Boxiness carve','Articulation lift','Proximity effect','Low-mid buildup','High-pass filter','Dialogue stem']::text[],ARRAY['Cutting so much low end that the voice becomes thin and loses natural warmth and authority.','Using a broad chest cut instead of a high-pass filter to remove genuine sub-bass rumble.','UNSAFE: Monitoring the dubbing stage at high SPL for long stretches while fine-tuning the mix risks permanent noise-induced hearing loss; keep exposure within OSHA limits (90 dBA time-weighted over 8 hours, 85 dBA action level) and take listening breaks.']::text[],ARRAY['Taming proximity-effect boom on a close-miced narrator.','Thinning a deep voice so it does not overpower music in the low mids.','Cleaning up a chest-resonant lav mic for better intelligibility.']::text[],'739bdba4-0fac-4455-9ee2-1a56096cab92'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='c30f307a-ced9-5a67-a203-482f9f18956a' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Chest reduction'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
